  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nWe propose a gradient-based optimization method to minimize C\u2085, building on the initial_h_values. This approach differs from the previous PSO by:\n- Directly computing exact gradients of the overlap integral\n- Using line search for adaptive step sizes\n- Maintaining feasibility through projection\n- Employing two-phase optimization: global exploration followed by local refinement\n\nThe key innovation is combining analytical gradients with feasibility-preserving updates, allowing us to efficiently navigate the nonconvex objective while respecting constraints.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nWe aim to minimize:\n\nC\u2085 = (2/n) * max_{k=1}^{2n-1} sum_{i=1}^{n - |k| + 1} h[i] * (1 - h[i + |k| - 1])\n\nSubject to:\n- 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1\n- sum(h) = n/2\n\nThe gradient for lag k* is:\n\n\u2207h C\u2085 = (2/n) * [ (1 - h_{shifted}) - h ]_positive_overlap\n\nWhere positive_overlap indicates the region where both h and shifted h are non-zero.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\na) Initialization\n1. Load initial_h_values or initialize randomly\n2. Scale to ensure sum(h) = n/2\n3. Compute initial C\u2085\n\nb) Optimization loop\n1. Compute all correlation lags\n2. Identify maximum lag k*\n3. Compute gradient for k*\n4. Perform line search for step size\n5. Update h: h = h - \u03b1 * gradient\n6. Project h onto feasible region\n7. Compute new C\u2085\n8. Adapt \u03b1 based on progress\n9. Repeat until budget exhausted or convergence\n\nc) Gradient computation\n1. For lag k*, determine overlap indices\n2. Compute component-wise gradient contributions\n3. Weight by 2/n factor\n\nd) Projection\n1. Clip values to [0,1]\n2. Rescale to sum(h)=n/2 using proportionate adjustment\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n- n_points = 100 (same as initial_h_values)\n- Initial learning rate \u03b1 = 1e-3\n- Line search factor: 0.5\n- Gradient threshold for stagnation: 1e-6\n- Maximum iterations: 5000\n- Random perturbation fraction: 0.01\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nEach iteration involves O(n) operations for gradient and projection. With n=100 and 5000 iterations, total operations are 5e5, easily within 600s budget.\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n- Ensure 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1\n- Verify sum(h) = n/2\n- Compute C\u2085 using evaluator\n- Return best solution",
